zontal or\nvertical segment of \\(P\\); that segment contains an uncovered square of \\(H\\).\nSince a tile may not cover any square of \\(H\\), such a rectangle cannot exist.\n∎\n\nConsequently every tile lies wholly in \\(A\\) or wholly in \\(B\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.** *A tile that lies in \\(A\\) can intersect at most one vertical\nsegment of \\(P\\).*  \n*Proof.* Suppose a tile \\(T\\subset A\\) intersected two distinct vertical\nsegments, say those at columns \\(c_1<c_2\\).  Between those vertical segments\n\\(P\\) contains a horizontal segment at some row \\(r\\) joining \\((c_1,r)\\) to\n\\((c_2,r)\\).  Since \\(T\\) is a rectangle, its interior contains the whole\nhorizontal strip between the two vertical sides; therefore it would intersect\nthat horizontal segment, which contains the uncovered square \\((r,c_1)\\) or\n\\((r,c_2)\\).  This contradicts Lemma 1. ∎\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) vertical segments of \\(P\\); by Lemma 2 each tile in\n\\(A\\) can “cover’’ at most one of them, so at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed\nto cover all of \\(A\\).\n\nAn analogous argument (using symmetry) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are\nrequired to cover \\(B\\).\n\nHence any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimal number of tiles\n\nThe construction of Section 2 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the\nargument of Section 3 proves that no smaller number is possible.  Therefore the\nminimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nMatilda can place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal and then tile\nthe remaining squares with the \\(n-1\\) rectangles above the diagonal and the\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles below it, using a total of \\(4048\\) tiles.  No arran