t be covered by tiles.  \nThus we must partition the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\; i\\neq j\\}\n\\]\n\ninto axis‑aligned rectangles that contain no diagonal cell.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Two disjoint triangular regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT^{+}= \\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\qquad\\text{(upper triangle)},\n\\]\n\\[\nT^{-}= \\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\qquad\\text{(lower triangle)}.\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(C = T^{+}\\cup T^{-}\\) and \\(T^{+}\\cap T^{-}=\\varnothing\\).\n\n*Claim.* A rectangle that contains points from both \\(T^{+}\\) and \\(T^{-}\\) must also contain a diagonal point.  \n\n*Proof.* Let a rectangle have row interval \\([r_{1},r_{2}]\\) and column interval \\([c_{1},c_{2}]\\).  \nIf it contains a point \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) and a point \\((k,\\ell)\\) with \\(k>\\ell\\), then \\(r_{1}\\le i<j\\le c_{2}\\) and \\(r_{1}\\le k\\le r_{2}\\), \\(c_{1}\\le \\ell<k\\le r_{2}\\). Hence the intervals overlap and the rectangle also contains the point \\((m,m)\\) where \\(m\\) is any integer satisfying \\(\\max(r_{1},c_{1})\\le m\\le\\min(r_{2},c_{2})\\); in particular the diagonal cell with \\(m=\\max(r_{1},c_{1})\\) lies inside. ∎  \n\nTherefore every tile lies entirely in either \\(T^{+}\\) or \\(T^{-}\\).  \nConsequently the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\nN = N^{+}+N^{-},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(N^{+}\\) (resp. \\(N^{-}\\)) is the number of rectangles used to cover \\(T^{+}\\) (resp. \\(T^{-}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lower bound for one triangle  \n\nConsider the cells just above the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nS^{+}= \\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subset T^{+}.\n\\]\n\n*Lemma.* No rectangle can contain two distinct cells of \\(S^{+}\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nIts row interval must contain \\(\\{i,j\\}\\) and its column interval must contain \\(\\{i+1,j+1\\}\\).  \nHence