e i+1 ≤ j and l+1 ≤ k. Then i+1 ≤ j ≤ c2 => i+1 ≤ c2. Also l+1 ≤ k ≤ r2 => l+1 ≤ r2. Also i ≥ r1 and l ≥ c1. So r1 ≤ i < j ≤ c2, and c1 ≤ l < k ≤ r2. So r1 ≤ i < c2 and c1 ≤ l < r2. So r1 ≤ c2 and c1 ≤ r2. So max(r1,c1) ≤ min(r2,c2). So intervals intersect. So there is an integer m in the intersection. Then (m,m) is inside rectangle. So proof is correct.\n\nThus the claim is valid.\n\n- Then they claim \"Hence every tile lies entirely in either T+ or T-.\" That's correct.\n\n- Then they define S+ = {(i,i+1)}. They prove Lemma 1: No rectangle can contain two distinct cells of S+. The proof: Suppose rectangle contains (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with i<j. Then row interval includes i and j; column interval includes i+1 and j+1. Since i+1 ≤ j, rectangle also contains (i+1,i+1) diagonal cell. That's correct.\n\n- Then they claim \"Since each rectangle can cover at most one element of S+ and |S+| = n-1, we need at least n-1 rectangles to cover all of T+.\" Wait: This step uses that each rectangle can cover at most one S+ cell, and there are n-1 S+ cells. But does covering all of T+ require covering all S+ cells? Yes, because S+ is a subset of T+. So any covering of T+ must cover S+. So we need at least n-1 rectangles to cover S+, thus at least n-1 rectangles to cover T+. This is correct.\n\n- Similarly for lower triangle.\n\n- Then they construct tiling with exactly n-1 rectangles for each triangle: For each i, rectangle R_i^+ = {(i,j) | j=i+1,...,n}. That's a rectangle covering row i from column i+1 to n. That's a rectangle (i,i) row interval and column interval [i+1,n]. That's valid. They claim these rectangles are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows. Indeed, each rectangle occupies a distinct row i. So they don't overlap. Their union is precisely T+. Indeed, T+ consists of all cells (i,j) with i<j. For each i, the rectangle covers all j>i. So union over i covers all i<j. So that's correct.\n\n- Similarly for lower triangle: For each i, rectangle R_i^- = {(j,i)