e, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)\\).  \n   - For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nDefine the two regions of covered squares\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the whole set of covered squares is the disjoint union \\(L\\cup R\\).  \nA *tile* is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists entirely of covered squares; tiles are required to be pairwise disjoint and to cover \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Lower bound for the left region)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_i=\\sigma(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((i,c_i)\\) is the right‑most covered cell of row \\(i\\).  \nLet \\(T\\) be a tile that contains \\((i,c_i)\\). Because a tile is a rectangle, its set of columns is an interval \\([a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le c_i\\le b\\).  \nIf \\(b>c_i\\) then column \\(c_i+1\\) would also belong to the tile, but column \\(c_i+1=\\sigma(i)\\) is the uncovered square of row \\(i\\); this contradicts the definition of a tile.  \nHence \\(b=c_i\\); the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(c_i\\).\n\nThe numbers \\(c_i\\) (for those rows with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\)) are precisely the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\), each appearing once because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nConsequently each integer \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) must be the rightmost column of a distinct tile covering the cell \\((i,k)\\) where \\(\\sigma(i)-1=k\\). 