he tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint, any admissible tiling of the entire board uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nT_k^{L}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i\\ge k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T_k^{L}\\) is a vertical strip of width one column, extending from row \\(k+1\\) down to row \\(n\\).  Because for every row \\(i\\ge k+1\\) we have \\(i>k\\), the cells \\((i,k)\\) belong to the left region.  The strips \\(\\{T_k^{L}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nT_k^{R}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i\\le k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T_k^{R}\\) is a vertical strip of width one column, extending from row \\(1\\) up to row \\(k-1\\).  Since for every row \\(i\\le k-1\\) we have \\(i<k\\), the cells \\((i,k)\\) belong to the right region.  The strips \\(\\{T_k^{R}\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) are disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(R\\).\n\nThus the collection\n\\[\n\\{T_k^{L}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{T_k^{R}\\}_{k=2}^{n}\n\\]\nconsists of exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles, each a valid tile, they are pairwise disjoint, and they cover every covered square while leaving precisely the diagonal squares uncovered.  \n\nTherefore a tiling with \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Self-Correction Instruction ###**  \nBefore finalizing, verify that every statement in the \u201cSummary\