a(i))\\).\n\nDefine the two subsets of covered squares\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThus the covered squares are exactly \\(L\\cup R\\), and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\nA rectangle (tile) cannot contain squares from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\): if a rectangle contained a cell \\((i,j)\\in L\\) and a cell \\((i',j')\\in R\\), then its column interval would contain \\(\\sigma(i)\\) for the row \\(i\\) (or \\(\\sigma(i')\\) for the row \\(i'\\)), forcing the rectangle to include an uncovered square, which is forbidden. Hence every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the left region  \n\nConsider a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\).  The cell\n\\[\nc_i:=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\n\\]\nis the right‑most covered cell of that row.  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be a tile that contains \\(c_i\\).  Since \\(T\\) is a rectangle, its set of columns is an interval \\([a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le \\sigma(i)-1\\le b\\).  If \\(b>\\sigma(i)-1\\), then column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) would also belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the fact that \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is uncovered.  Therefore\n\\[\nb=\\sigma(i)-1.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the rightmost column of the tile covering \\(c_i\\) is uniquely determined as \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) for those rows with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) constitute exactly the set\n\\[\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\neach appearing once.  Consequently each integer \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) must be the rightmost column of a distinct tile (the tile that covers the unique cell \\((i,k)\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)-1=k\\)).  Hence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover the whole region \\(L\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the right region  \n\nNow consider a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\).  The cell\n\\[\nd_i:=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\n\\]\nis the left‑most covered cell of that row.  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be a tile containing \\(d_i\\).  Its column interval is \\([a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le \\sigma(i)+1\\le b\\).  If \\(a<\\sigma(i)+1\\), then column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) would also belong to \\(T\\), again contradicting the uncovered status of \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  Hence\n\\[\na=\\sigma(i)+1.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the leftmost column of the tile covering \\(d_i\\) equals \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\).\n\nThe values \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) for rows with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) are precisely the set\n\\[\n\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\neach occurring once.  Therefore each integer \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) must be the leftmost column of a distinct tile, and at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover the region \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Global lower bound  \n\nSince the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint, any admissible tiling of the entire board uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nT_k^{L}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i\\ge k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T_k^{L}\\) is a vertical strip of width one column, extending from row \\(k+1\\) down to row \\(n\\).  Because for every row \\(i\\ge k+1\\) we have \\(i>k\\), the cells \\((i,k)\\) belong to the left region.  The strips \\(\\{T_k^{L}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each column \\(k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nT_k^{R}=\\{(i,k)\\mid i\\le k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T_k^{R}\\) is a vertical strip of width one column, extending from row \\(1\\) up to row \\(k-1\\).  Since for every row \\(i\\le k-1\\) we have \\(i<k\\), the cells \\((i,k)\\) belong to the right region.  The strips \\(\\{T_k^{R}\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) are disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(R\\).\n\nThus the collection\n\\[\n\\{T_k^{L}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{T_k^{R}\\}_{k=2}^{n}\n\\]\nconsists of exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles, each a valid tile, they are pairwise disjoint, and they cover every covered square while leaving precisely the diagonal squares uncovered.  \n\nTherefore a tiling with \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Self-Correction Instruction ###**  \nBefore finalizing, verify that every statement in the “Summary” and “Detailed Solution” is mathematically rigorous, that all needed definitions (e.g., permutation matrix, left/right regions) are provided, and that no step relies on unproven intuition.  Ensure all mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters and that the final answer appears in \\(\\boxed{}\\) form.  Confirm that the lower‑bound lemmas correctly account for rows with \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) or \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) and that the construction indeed covers all cells of the left and right regions without overlap.  After this check, output the solution exactly as written above.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final