wn}}$ is empty. In the two columns containing the holes of rows $1$ and $n$ only one strip is needed; in all other $n-2$ columns we need both strips.  \n   * Hence the total number of rectangles is  \n     \\[\n       2\\,(n-2)+2 = 2n-2 .\n     \\]  \n   These $2n-2$ vertical strips are pairwise disjoint and together cover every unit square except the $n$ uncovered ones.  \n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound $2n-2$ and the explicit construction with exactly $2n-2$ tiles coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2=4048$ for $n=2025$.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet $n=2025$.  \nFor each row $i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)$ denote by $\\pi(i)$ the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, $\\pi$ is a permutation of $\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of $2n-2$\n\nFor $k=1,\\dots ,n-1$ let $r_{k+1}$ be the unique row with $\\pi(r_{k+1})=k+1$.  \nDefine the cell  \n\n\\[\nA_k=(r_{k+1},\\,k).\n\\]\n\nBecause $k<\\pi(r_{k+1})$, the cell $A_k$ lies **to the left** of the hole in its row; hence $A_k$ must be covered by a tile.  \nMoreover $A_k$ does **not** occur in column $k+1$, for column $k+1$ contains the hole $(r_{k+1},k+1)$.  \n\nLet $R$ be any rectangle (tile) that covers $A_k$.  \nWrite its column interval as $[a,b]$ with $1\\le a\\le b\\le n$.  \nSince $A_k$ is covered, $a\\le k\\le b$.  \nIf $b\\ge k+1$, then column $k+1$ belongs to $R$; but row $r_{k+1}$ contains the hole $(r_{k+1},k+1)$, so $R$ would also contain that hole – a contradiction.  \nHence $b\\le k$.  Because $k\\in[a,b]$, we must have $b=k$.  \n\nThus **every** rectangle covering $A_k$ has rightmost column exactly $k$.  \nTwo different values of $k$ give disjoint families of rectangles, because a rectangle has a unique rightmost column.  \nConsequently the $n-1$ cells $A_1,\\dots ,A_{n-1}$ force at least $n-1$ distinct rectangles.\n\nA completely symmetric argument yields another $n-1$ forced rectangles.  \nFor $k=2,\\dots ,n$ let $s_{k-1}$ be the unique row with $\\pi(s_{k-1})=k-1$ and set  \n\n\\[\nB_k=(s_{k-1},\\,k).\n\\]\n\nThe cell $B_k$ lies **to the right** of its hole and does not appear in column $k-1$.  \nIf a rectangle $R$ covers $B_k$, writing its column interval as $[a,b]$, the same reasoning shows that $a\\ge k$; hence $a=k$.  \nThus each $B_k$ forces a rectangle whose **leftmost** column equals $k$, and again the $n-1$ values $k=2,\\dots ,n$ give $n-1$ distinct rectangles.\n\nA rectangle cannot simultaneously have rightmost column $k$ and leftmost column $\\ell$ with $k\\neq\\ell$, so the two families of forced rectangles are disjoint.  \nTherefore any tiling of the board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with $2n-2$ tiles\n\nFor each column $c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)$ let $r_c$ be the unique row with $\\pi(r_c)=c$ (the hole of column $c$).  \nConsider the two vertical strips\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_c^{\\text{up}}   &=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i< r_c\\},\\\\[2mm]\nR_c^{\\text{down}} &=\\{(i,c)\\mid r_c< i\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth $R_c^{\\text{up}}$ and $R_c^{\\text{down}}$ are rectangles: they have width $1$ (column $c$) and a contiguous set of rows.  \nEvery unit square that is not a hole belongs to exactly one of these strips, because for any non‑hole cell $(i,c)$ we have either $c<\\pi(i)$ (so $i<r_c$ and the cell lies in $R_c^{\\text{up}}$) or $c>\\pi(i)$ (so $i>r_c$ and the cell lies in $R_c^{\\text{down}}$).\n\nIf the hole of column $c$ lies in the top row ($r_c=1$) then $R_c^{\\text{up}}$ is empty; if it lies in the bottom row ($r_c=n$) then $R_c^{\\text{down}}$ is empty.  \nExactly two columns have this property: the column containing the hole of row $1$ and the column containing the hole of row $n$.  \nHence we need\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2\\,(n-2)}_{\\text{both strips in the $n-2$ interior columns}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{one strip in each of the two boundary columns}}\n\\;=\\;2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles to cover the whole board.\n\nAll these rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every unit square except the $n$ uncovered ones.  Thus a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound $2n-2$ and the explicit construction with exactly $2n-2$ tiles coincide.  \nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
