"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there exists a bijection  \n   \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)) such that the unique uncovered\n   square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \n\n2. **Decomposition into two regions.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \n   B=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\}.\n   \\]\n   Every tile must lie entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\) because a tile may not contain an uncovered square.\n\n3. **Correct lower bound for region\u202f\\(A\\).**  \n   For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq n\\) (i.e. with at least one cell in \\(A\\)) consider the **leftmost** cell of \\(A\\) in that row,\n   \\[\n   a_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n   \\]\n   There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells, because precisely one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) and then \\(\\ell_i=n-\\sigma(i)=0\\).  \n\n   **Lemma.** No axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(A\\) can contain two distinct cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_k\\).  \n   *Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq A\\) contains \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_k\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\).  \n   Without loss assume \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(k)\\).  \n   Then the column interval of \\(R\\) contains both columns \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(k)+1\\); consequently it also contains the intermediate column \\(\\sigma(k)\\). Hence \\(R\\) contains the cell \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\), which is the uncovered square of row \\(k\\) and does **not** belong to \\(A\\) \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e  \n\n   Because each of the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(a_i\\) must lie in a distinct rectangle, any covering of \\(A\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Symmetric lower bound for region\u202f\\(B\\).**  \n   Rotating the board by \\(90^\\circ\\) interchanges the roles of rows and columns and swaps \\(A\\) with \\(B\\). The same argument applied to the rightmost cell of each column of \\(B\\) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover \\(B\\).\n\n5. **Upper bound (construction).**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal.  \n   - For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering all cells of \\(A\\) in that row.  \n   - For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile covering all cells of \\(B\\) in that column.  \n\n   The families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, none of them contains a diagonal cell, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal unit square.  The total number of tiles is  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower\u2011bound argument shows that at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary; the construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) exactly one unit square is left uncovered; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.  The set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the two complementary regions  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery unit square of the board belongs to exactly one of the three sets\n\\(U\\), \\(A\\), \\(B\\).  A rectangular tile may not contain any point of \\(U\\); therefore each tile lies wholly inside \\(A\\) or wholly inside \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Lower bound for the number of tiles covering \\(A\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq n\\) (i.e. with at least one cell of \\(A\\)) define  \n\n\\[\na_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1),\n\\]\n\nthe leftmost cell of \\(A\\) in that row.  Since \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); consequently the set \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) contains precisely \\(n-1\\) distinct cells.\n\n**Lemma.** No axis\u2011parallel rectangle \\(R\\subseteq A\\) can contain two distinct cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_k\\).\n\n*Proof.* Assume, to the contrary, that such a rectangle \\(R\\) exists.  Without loss of generality suppose \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(k)\\).  Because \\(R\\) contains \\(a_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) and \\(a_k=(k,\\sigma(k)+1)\\), the column interval of \\(R\\) includes both columns \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(k)+1\\); therefore it also contains the intermediate column \\(\\sigma(k)\\) (since \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\le\\sigma(k)<\\sigma(k)+1\\)).  Hence the cell \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  But \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\) is an uncovered square, i.e. it does not belong to \\(A\\).  This contradicts the assumption \\(R\\subseteq A\\).  \u220e\n\nBecause each of the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(a_i\\) must lie in a distinct rectangle, any covering of \\(A\\) by axis\u2011parallel rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the number of tiles covering \\(B\\)\n\nRotate the board by \\(90^\\circ\\).  This transformation interchanges rows and columns and swaps the roles of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Applying the argument of Lemma\u202f1 to the rightmost cell of each column of \\(B\\) yields the analogous statement: at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover \\(B\\).\n\nConsequently any admissible tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tiles covering \\(A\\).*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.  The rectangles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(A\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(B\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile.  The rectangles \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all cells of \\(B\\).\n\nNo rectangle contains any diagonal cell, and every non\u2011diagonal unit square belongs to exactly one of the rectangles \\(H_i\\) or \\(V_j\\).  The total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nBoth sides of the inequality are tight: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary, and \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
